  {
    slug: "304-openai-short-egyptian-fractions-refereed",
    problemNum: 304,
    title: "#304: every a/b is a sum of O(log log b) distinct unit fractions — OpenAI's proof of Erdős's 1950 conjecture, with log log F(k) ≍ k (#148) and log log v(k) ≍ k (#293), read by AI seats here, its Lean replayed and bridged to Formal Conjectures",
    date: "2026-10-21",
    status: "proof_claimed",
    originatedByUs: false,
    summary:
      "Let N(b) be the largest number of distinct unit fractions, all with denominators at least 2, that some a/b with 1 ≤ a < b needs. In 1950 Erdős proved log log b ≪ N(b) ≪ log b/log log b and asked whether N(b) ≪ log log b; Vose improved the upper bound to √(log b) in 1985. This is Erdős problem #304. OpenAI's 6 October release proves the conjecture. Its route is a descent. After O(log log b) greedy steps the remainder is A/C with C in a fixed exponential range. The paper then builds one auxiliary denominator M from products of primes near (log b)¹⁰⁰ and shows that almost every fraction u/(MC) with u below a fixed height has an expansion of length O(log log b). Each step peels off one unit fraction 1/((M/t)z), with t a divisor of M chosen so that the leftover numerator is small, and a uniform divisor moment with Hölder's inequality keeps the exceptional numerators sparse at every level. A scaled copy of A·M is then the sum of two good numerators. The same paper settles the order of the double logarithm in two neighbouring problems: the number F(k) of k-term expansions of 1 (#148) and the least integer v(k) ≥ 2 that is never a denominator in one (#293) both have log log of order k. @@READSUMMARY@@. OpenAI's Lean has two separate developments, 348 modules in all, which we replayed with the standard axioms and matching statements. Our kernel-checked bridges carry the results to verbatim copies of Formal Conjectures' statements for #304, to FC's own counting function for #148, and to a statement for #293, which FC does not yet have. Nothing in the theorems is ours.",
    body: `
<p><strong>The problems.</strong> <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/304">Erdős problem #304</a> [ErGr80, p. 37]: "For integers 1 ≤ a &lt; b let N(a,b) denote the minimal k such that there exist integers 1 &lt; n<sub>1</sub> &lt; ⋯ &lt; n<sub>k</sub> with a/b = 1/n<sub>1</sub> + ⋯ + 1/n<sub>k</sub>. Estimate N(b) = max<sub>1≤a&lt;b</sub> N(a,b). Is it true that N(b) ≪ log log b?" Erdős [Er50c] proved log log b ≪ N(b) ≪ log b/log log b, the lower bound already for a = b − 1, and Vose [Vo85] improved the upper bound to N(b) ≪ √(log b). Two neighbours share the paper. <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/148">#148</a> [ErGr80, p. 32] asks for good estimates of F(k), the number of solutions of 1 = 1/n<sub>1</sub> + ⋯ + 1/n<sub>k</sub> with 1 ≤ n<sub>1</sub> &lt; ⋯ &lt; n<sub>k</sub>; the page gives Konyagin's lower bound log log F(k) ≫ k/log k [Ko14] and the Elsholtz–Planitzer upper bound F(k) ≤ c<sub>0</sub><sup>(1/5+o(1))2<sup>k</sup></sup> [ElPl21]. <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/293">#293</a> [ErGr80, p. 35] asks for the growth of v(k), the least integer that does not occur as a denominator in such a k-term solution. Taken literally that is 1 for every k ≥ 2, and the page reads it, after a December 2025 comment on its thread, as the least such integer above 1. Van Doorn and Tang [vDTa25b] proved log v(k) ≫ k² and noted the link to #304 through N(b−1, b). When we fetched them on 21 October, all three pages pointed to the same 14 October exposition of OpenAI's proof by Bloom and van Doorn (<a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/static/UnitFractions.pdf">UnitFractions.pdf</a>). None had a proof claim, and new comments were suspended. Our earlier work on all three is from 26 July: <a href="/report/304">/report/304</a> (an exhaustive search proving N(b) ≤ 7 for b ≤ 1000, with equality exactly at b = 733, 739, 787, 839, 863, 898 and 907), <a href="/report/148">/report/148</a> (F(1), …, F(7) recomputed) and <a href="/report/293">/report/293</a> (v(1), …, v(7) and 2307 ≤ v(8) ≤ 27539; the forum has since found v(8) = 27,539 and v(9) = 13,856,993).</p>

<p><strong>The claim.</strong> OpenAI's family 25, released on 6 October, is one preprint, "Short Egyptian fractions" (25 September, 33 pp). Theorem 1.1: there are absolute constants c<sub>1</sub>, c<sub>2</sub> &gt; 0 and b<sub>0</sub> such that c<sub>1</sub> log log b ≤ N(b) ≤ c<sub>2</sub> log log b for every b ≥ b<sub>0</sub>. Corollary 1.2: ck ≤ log log F(k) ≤ Ck for every large k. Corollary 1.3: exp(exp(k/600)) ≤ v(k) ≤ 1 + k<sup>2<sup>k−1</sup></sup> for every large k, and more precisely log 2/257 ≤ lim inf log log v(k)/k ≤ lim sup log log v(k)/k ≤ log 2. The paper does not compute c<sub>2</sub>. The lower bound in Theorem 1.1 is Erdős's, and the upper bounds in the corollaries are elementary. The new content is the uniform upper bound for N(b), the lower bound log log F(k) ≫ k, which improves Konyagin's k/log k, and the lower bound for v(k), which improves van Doorn and Tang's exp(ck²).</p>

<p><strong>The argument.</strong> Put S = log b. Section 2 reduces everything to one proposition. A greedy prefix of O(log S) terms leaves a remainder A/C with A &lt; b and e<sup>D<sub>C</sub>S</sup> ≤ C &lt; e<sup>2D<sub>C</sub>S</sup> (Lemma 2.3). Proposition 2.1 then supplies an integer M with e<sup>S</sup> &lt; M ≤ e<sup>D<sub>M</sub>S</sup> and a set G of numerators u ≤ X = e<sup>D<sub>X</sub>S</sup>, missing at most X/8 of them, such that every u/(MC) with u in G is a sum of at most L log S unit fractions. Take g = ⌊X/(AM)⌋. Of the splits gAM = u + (gAM − u), at most X/4 touch a missing numerator, so one split has both parts in G. Adding the two expansions gives gA/C, and multiplying every denominator by g gives A/C. Takenouchi's 1921 replacements 2/(2p) = 1/(p+1) + 1/(p(p+1)) and 2/(2p+1) = 1/(p+1) + 1/((p+1)(2p+1)) then make the whole list distinct without lengthening it (Lemma 2.2). Sections 3–5 prove the proposition. M is the product of a power of 2 at least S<sup>100000</sup> and of primes in [S<sup>100</sup>, 2S<sup>100</sup>]: m = ⌊S/log S⌋ fixed ones, whose 2<sup>m</sup> subset products form a deterministic list of divisors, and 1000 blocks of independently sampled ones, which form random lists. A divisor t of M gives the identity u/(MQ) = 1/((M/t)z) + (1/z)·h/(MQ), with z = ⌈Qt/u⌉ and h = uz − Qt the least residue of −Qt modulo u. One unit fraction thus replaces the numerator u by h. The descent runs through levels X<sub>j</sub> = X·e<sup>−10⁻⁴mj</sup>, at most 3D<sub>X</sub>·10⁴·log S of them (about 6·10⁹·log S with the paper's constants). At each level, Fourier discrepancy estimates (Section 4) show that for all but an e<sup>−0.001m</sup> fraction of u, at least a ρ/2 share of the listed divisors give h below the next level. The danger is that h lands on an earlier failure. Every predecessor of a given pair (h, t) divides Qt + h, so Section 3's uniform bound Σ<sub>h≤Y</sub> d<sub>X</sub>(N+h)<sup>r</sup> ≤ Y·exp(S<sup>1/4</sup>) on the r-th moment of the truncated divisor function, for every shift N ≤ e<sup>DS</sup>, controls them all at once. With Hölder's inequality it gives the recurrence δ<sub>j</sub> ≤ e<sup>−0.001m</sup> + e<sup>2S<sup>1/4</sup></sup>δ<sub>j+1</sub><sup>1−1/r</sup> for the failure density, which stays below 1/8 when unrolled over the d levels. Below e<sup>m</sup> the random lists reduce every numerator, and the binary expansion over the power of 2 in M finishes. For #148 (Section 6), apply the theorem to (Q−1)/Q with Q the product of the first r odd primes, append 1/Q, and split the denominator n that Q divides as 1/n = 1/(n+d) + 1/(n+n²/d) over its divisors d. This gives at least 2<sup>r−1</sup> distinct expansions of length O(log r); the padding 1/v = 1/(v+1) + 1/(v(v+1)) on the largest denominator is injective and brings every one to length k. For #293 (Sections 7–8), reserve 1/m and use a greedy prefix that skips m. Its remainder is smaller than every reserved reciprocal, so no expansion of it can reuse a reserved denominator. The explicit slope log 2/257 comes from a second construction built on a quantitative three-primes theorem.</p>

<p><strong>The Lean, and our replay.</strong> @@REPLAY@@ @@LEANCHECKER@@</p>

<p><strong>Two formalizations, and what they prove on the way.</strong> @@ROUTE@@</p>

<p><strong>Formal Conjectures, and the bridges (ours).</strong> @@BRIDGE@@</p>

<p><strong>The reading.</strong> @@READING@@</p>

<p><strong>Small cases (ours, computational).</strong> @@EMPIRICAL@@</p>

<p><strong>Sources and the world.</strong> @@WORLD@@</p>

<p><strong>What this entry is.</strong> A referee report on someone else's proof. The theorems, their proofs and their Lean formalizations are OpenAI's, and Bloom and van Doorn's exposition preceded us by a week. Ours: the replay and its controls, the three bridges and their controls, the readings, and the small-case computations. In our labels, the paper's proof is (b), rigorous modulo the cited prime-number-theorem estimates. The Lean proves its own versions of what the paper cites, including a three-primes theorem, so the formal statements depend on nothing beyond Mathlib. The Lean is a machine check of the theorems as stated; it is OpenAI's own, and it is silent on whether the printed text is right. @@RECEIPTS@@</p>
`,
